<div class="publications">
  {%- comment -%}
    Order here is deliberate, not computed. jekyll-scholar has no --sort_by
    switch (an unknown switch is split into a bare "--", which OptionParser
    reads as "end of options" and silently drops everything after it), and it
    applies the global scholar.sort_by from _config.yml to every bibliography
    tag — including one restricted to a --file. That sort is "year, title",
    so a single tag here lists the newest year first and then alphabetically
    by title within it, which is not an order anyone chose.

    So each slot is pulled by hand instead. `rank` is a custom field on the
    entries in selected.bib; to reorder the landing page, renumber it there.
    Entries ranked above 5 are simply not rendered, so the file doubles as the
    priority list for what moves up next.

    Every entry in selected.bib is an @article, and --query @article is the
    form already proven on the publications page, so the condition is narrowed
    onto that rather than onto @*.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% bibliography --file selected --query @article[rank=1] --group_by none %}
  {% bibliography --file selected --query @article[rank=2] --group_by none %}
  {% bibliography --file selected --query @article[rank=3] --group_by none %}
  {% bibliography --file selected --query @article[rank=4] --group_by none %}
  {% bibliography --file selected --query @article[rank=5] --group_by none %}
</div>
